{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.linker.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\t# yin.vm de Bruijn linker over dao.jing (B6 and R5)\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: design, extracted from the stack and register designs; not\n     4\timplemented\n     5\t\n     6\tThis document specifies the committed closed-image linker for the two de\n     7\tBruijn executable formats of `yin.vm`: the stack image, identified by H,\n     8\tand the register image, identified by R. It is the standalone home of what\n     9\tthe stack design (`yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md`) calls phase B6 and what the\n    10\tregister design (`yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`) calls phase R5. Those two\n    11\tphases are one unit of work: one fetch function, parameterized by a format\n    12\trecord, with two concrete format records. The phase headers and file boxes\n    13\tremain in their parent designs so each phase list stays complete; the\n    14\tspecification lives here.\n    15\t\n    16\t## 1. Purpose and context\n    17\t\n    18\tThe stack design's governing invariant I says: a `yin.vm` can share\n    19\texecutable code over a `dao.stream` linker. The linker may be local or\n    20\tremote, but its boundary is always the stream. The linker is the component\n    21\tthat discharges that invariant. Everything upstream of it, the resolver\n    22\t(B2), the stack lowerer (B2), the register lowerer (R1), and the two\n    23\texecutable dimensions with their hash functions (B1 and R1), produces a\n    24\tcontent-addressed image and its identity. The linker is what lets a second\n    25\thost, holding only that identity, obtain the image, prove it is the one\n    26\tasked for, prove it is well formed, prove it is closed for the receiving\n    27\tenvironment, and only then hand it to a VM.\n    28\t\n    29\tThree facts from the parent designs are needed to read this document:\n    30\t\n    31\t1. H is B1's `image-hash` over the stack descriptor hash, which includes\n    32\t   the lowering-contract version, and the canonical positional stack\n    33\t   instruction vector. R is R1's `register-hash` over the register\n    34\t   descriptor hash and the canonical register vector. Both are pinned to\n    35\t   explicit SHA-256 by VM contract freeze and are independent of DaoJing's\n    36\t   `default-hash-algorithm`. Binder names are outside both preimages, so\n    37\t   both are alpha-invariant for binder renames. Neither preimage mentions\n    38\t   the other; H and R are peer format identities, not competing semantic\n    39\t   identities.\n    40\t2. An image is closed for a receiver when every `:load-free` operand\n    41\t   resolves through the receiver's primitive or module tables and none is\n    42\t   shadowed by the receiver's free environment or store (stack design D11,\n    43\t   D15). An image with no `:load-free` operands is closed trivially. Until\n    44\t   B7 supplies dependency closure, only closed images are safely shared.\n    45\t3. A fetched image may be executed by the existing named semantic VM after\n    46\t   lifting to `:yin.code/*` with synthesized names (stack design D13). The\n    47\t   linker therefore depends on neither the stack kernel (B3, B4) nor the\n    48\t   register kernel (R4), and is on the shortest path to sharing: B0, B1,\n    49\t   B2, then this linker.\n    50\t\n    51\tThe key design decision is that there is one linker, not two. Fetch is one\n    52\tfunction of a Jing handle, an index, and a format record. The stack design\n    53\tcontributes the function and the stack format record; the register design\n    54\tcontributes the register format record. The register path is the second\n    55\tformat of the same function, not a second linker or a mirror. The two\n    56\tformats are built together as one unit because a function's first two\n    57\tparameterizations are best written against each other.\n    58\t\n    59\tEverything in this document obeys the `datom.world.md` invariants: no\n    60\thidden global state (the index and the handle are composition values), no\n    61\tcallbacks (absence is a parked request on a stream, never a loader\n    62\tinvocation), no shared mutable state, and no collapse of layers (Jing never\n    63\tlearns what an image is; the linker never computes a second address).\n    64\t\n    65\t## 2. Relation to dao.jing\n    66\t\n    67\tThe linker is an application of `dao.jing` over `dao.stream`, per the\n    68\towner's ruling. Jing is passive and payload-agnostic syntax; the linker is\n    69\tone of its interpreters.\n    70\t\n    71\t### 2.1 What dao.jing already provides\n    72\t\n    73\t    +---------------------------+---------------------------------------+\n    74\t    | Capability                | Where                                 |\n    75\t    +---------------------------+---------------------------------------+\n    76\t    | Content address minting   | dao.jing/segment-key: the digest of   |\n    77\t    |                           | the order-normalized print of a       |\n    78\t    |                           | value, spelled :segment/<algo>-<hex>  |\n    79\t    | Store and read by address | dao.jing/materialize!, dao.jing/get,  |\n    80\t    |                           | over any handle carrying              |\n    81\t    |                           | :put-content-fn and :get-content-fn   |\n    82\t    | Address-payload check     | dao.jing/segment-matches?, a total    |\n    83\t    |                           | predicate, never throws               |\n    84\t    | Fetch by content address  | dao.jing.dht/create-content-dht:      |\n    85\t    | with verify-before-trust  | local read first, then peer lookup    |\n    86\t    |                           | nearest the hash over IDhtNet, fetch, |\n    87\t    |                           | segment-matches? of the payload       |\n    88\t    |                           | against the requested address before  |\n    89\t    |                           | acceptance, re-materialize locally,   |\n    90\t    |                           | mismatch refused                      |\n    91\t    | Transport over DaoStream  | dao.jing.remote: content-client wraps |\n    92\t    |                           | an RPC client as a Jing handle;       |\n    93\t    |                           | default-handlers serves put and get   |\n    94\t    |                           | from a local handle on the server     |\n    95\t    |                           | side; both ride the DaoStream v2 core |\n    96\t    | Closed-store, unreachable | Handled inside dht and remote; the    |\n    97\t    | peer, malformed envelope  | linker inherits their outcomes        |\n    98\t    +---------------------------+---------------------------------------+\n    99\t\n   100\tThe DHT has no roots, no names, no CAS records, no deletes, and no intake\n   101\tstreams by design. It routes registered content addresses and nothing\n   102\telse. This is why the index in section 6 is not Jing's job.\n   103\t\n   104\t### 2.2 What the linker adds\n   105\t\n   106\tPrecisely four things, plus tests:\n   107\t\n   108\t1. The format record shape (section 4.1), and the two concrete records\n   109\t   (section 5).\n   110\t2. The fetch function (section 4.2), one function over a Jing handle, an\n   111\t   index, and a format record.\n   112\t3. The H index and the R index (section 6), as a plain value or as datoms\n   113\t   the composition queries through its own store, and the same-root\n   114\t   pairing datoms (section 7).\n   115\t4. The qualified refusal vocabulary (section 4.3).\n   116\t\n   117\t### 2.3 What the linker does not add\n   118\t\n   119\tNo transport, no peer lookup, no cache, and no responder. The earlier\n   120\tresponder namespace that once sat in the B6 box is dropped; the server side\n   121\tis `dao.jing.remote`'s existing handlers. Addressing, peer lookup, transport\n   122\tover DaoStream, hash-before-trust at the DHT layer, local caching in the\n   123\tDHT's local handle, and closed-store, unreachable-peer, and malformed-\n   124\tenvelope handling are all inherited and none is reimplemented. Neither\n   125\tlayer is weakened or duplicated: Jing never learns what an image is, and\n   126\tthe linker adds no address computation beyond the one check in step 2.\n   127\t\n   128\t## 3. Address and identity: why H is not the address\n   129\t\n   130\tH (and R) is not the `dao.jing` address, and no translation makes it one.\n   131\tThe stack design's D9 states it: Jing addresses are storage locations, not\n   132\tH. The two values have different preimages and different owners:\n   133\t\n   134\t    address = (jing/segment-key image-value)\n   135\t    H       = (image-hash image-vector)          ; stack\n   136\t    R       = (register-hash register-image)     ; register\n   137\t\n   138\t`segment-key` is the address-carried algorithm's digest over the\n   139\torder-normalized print of a value. That print is a transitional encoder:\n   140\tevery minted address changes when the DaoJing CBOR encoding lands (section\n   141\t8). H is SHA-256 over B1's own exact byte encoding and the descriptor hash,\n   142\tand must not fork when the storage encoder does. R is the same for R1's\n   143\tencoding. The payload stored in Jing is the canonical image value itself,\n   144\texactly as `yin.vm.content/materialize-vector!` already stores `:yin.code/*`\n   145\tvectors, at its own `segment-key`. For the stack format that value is the\n   146\tcanonical instruction vector; for the register format it is the register\n   147\timage map, `{:bodies ... :instructions ...}`.\n   148\t\n   149\tThe pairing `[H address]` (or `[R address]`) is recorded by whoever\n   150\tpublishes the image. That pairing is the index of section 6.\n   151\t\n   152\tBecause the address and the identity are different functions of the same\n   153\tvalue, the fetch function runs two different checks on the received value\n   154\tand both are necessary:\n   155\t\n   156\t- Step 2 proves the value is the content at that address. This is\n   157\t  Jing's integrity rule, `segment-matches?`, and it is what\n   158\t  `yin.vm.content/fetch-vector` already does for named vectors.\n   159\t- Step 3 proves that content is the program the caller asked for. The\n   160\t  address alone cannot prove this, because the address is not H: an index\n   161\t  entry may be stale, swapped, or malicious, pointing a correct H at a\n   162\t  different but perfectly well-stored value. Only recomputing the format's\n   163\t  own hash over the received value closes that gap.\n   164\t\n   165\tStep 2 is the linker's own check, not the handle's. A `create-content-dht`\n   166\thandle verifies address against payload before returning a peer-fetched\n   167\tvalue, but a `content-client` handle, the RPC path over `dao.jing.remote`\n   168\tand the only cross-host path today, returns the value as received. The\n   169\tlinker checks on every topology so the guarantee never depends on which\n   170\thandle a composition wired.\n   171\t\n   172\tThere is no separate descriptor check. The descriptor hash, including the\n   173\tlowering-contract version, is inside H's preimage (D9, D14) and inside R's\n   174\t(register design section 3), and the stored payload carries no descriptor\n   175\tfield. A descriptor or version disagreement is therefore a `:hash-mismatch`\n   176\tat step 3.\n   177\t\n   178\t## 4. The fetch function\n   179\t\n   180\t### 4.1 Format record\n   181\t\n   182\tA format record is plain data:\n   183\t\n   184\t    {:format        <keyword>   ; the executable dimension being fetched\n   185\t     :hash-fn       <fn>        ; value -> identity (H or R)\n   186\t     :validate-fn   <fn>        ; value -> nil | qualified defect\n   187\t     :free-names-fn <fn>}       ; validated image -> set of free names\n   188\t\n   189\t`:hash-fn` is the format's own identity function, never `segment-key`.\n   190\t`:validate-fn` is the format's image validator and returns nil or a named\n   191\tdefect. `:free-names-fn` scans the validated image's `:load-free` operands\n   192\t(D15: free names are derived by scanning operands, not declared) and is\n   193\tcalled only after validation, so it need not be total over malformed\n   194\tvalues. A linker request names both the format and the identity, for\n   195\texample `{:format :yin.debruijn.register :hash R}`.\n   196\t\n   197\t### 4.2 The six steps\n   198\t\n   199\tFetch is one function of a Jing handle, an index, and a format record,\n   200\twritten here with H; R5 runs the identical steps with R in place of H:\n   201\t\n   202\t```clojure\n   203\t;; (fetch handle index format H)\n   204\t;; 1. address  <- (index H)\n   205\t;;               absent entry            -> :absent\n   206\t;; 2. value    <- (jing/get handle address absent)\n   207\t;;               absent value            -> :absent\n   208\t;;               (not= address (jing/segment-key value))\n   209\t;;                                       -> :address-mismatch\n   210\t;; 3.           (not= H ((:hash-fn format) value))\n   211\t;;                                       -> :hash-mismatch\n   212\t;; 4.           ((:validate-fn format) value) returns a defect\n   213\t;;                                       -> that qualified defect\n   214\t;; 5.           ((:free-names-fn format) value) against the receiver\n   215\t;;               unresolved name         -> :unresolved-free\n   216\t;;               name shadowed by free env or store\n   217\t;;                                       -> :shadowed-free\n   218\t;; 6. return the verified image\n   219\t```\n   220\t\n   221\tStep order is load-bearing. Address verification precedes identity\n   222\tverification because the two preimages differ (section 3). Validation\n   223\tprecedes the closure check so `:free-names-fn` only ever sees a well-formed\n   224\timage. The closure check precedes return so no unclosed image is ever\n   225\tloaded; D11 requires refusal, not execution, for an image the receiver\n   226\tcannot bind identically. For the register format, step 4 includes the\n   227\tregister design's section 4.5 live-set rules: a live-set defect in a\n   228\tfetched image is refused before load.\n   229\t\n   230\tStep 2's comparison uses the algorithm the address carries; the linker\n   231\tnever picks a hash algorithm of its own for the address check. Step 3's\n   232\tcomparison is the format's pinned SHA-256 and is unaffected by DaoJing's\n   233\tdefault.\n   234\t\n   235\t### 4.3 Refusal vocabulary\n   236\t\n   237\tEvery outcome is qualified data, never an exception and never a callback.\n   238\t\n   239\t    +---------------------+--------+----------------------------------------+\n   240\t    | Outcome             | Step   | Meaning                                |\n   241\t    +---------------------+--------+----------------------------------------+\n   242\t    | :absent             | 1 or 2 | The index has no entry for the         |\n   243\t    |                     |        | identity, or the address resolves to   |\n   244\t    |                     |        | no payload on any reachable store      |\n   245\t    | :address-mismatch   | 2      | The received value does not hash to    |\n   246\t    |                     |        | the address it was fetched from        |\n   247\t    | :hash-mismatch      | 3      | The value is the content at that       |\n   248\t    |                     |        | address but is not the program asked   |\n   249\t    |                     |        | for (includes descriptor or contract-  |\n   250\t    |                     |        | version disagreement)                  |\n   251\t    | validator defect    | 4      | The format's own named diagnostic,     |\n   252\t    |                     |        | including unsupported-value for a host |\n   253\t    |                     |        | lacking a scalar class and, for R, a   |\n   254\t    |                     |        | live-set defect                        |\n   255\t    | :unresolved-free    | 5      | A :load-free name resolves through     |\n   256\t    |                     |        | neither primitives nor modules         |\n   257\t    | :shadowed-free      | 5      | A :load-free name is shadowed by the   |\n   258\t    |                     |        | receiver's free environment or store   |\n   259\t    | :pairing-mismatch   | 7      | Verifying same-root fallback only      |\n   260\t    |                     |        | (section 7): recomputed H or R differs |\n   261\t    |                     |        | from the recorded pair                 |\n   262\t    +---------------------+--------+----------------------------------------+\n   263\t\n   264\tInherited outcomes from `dao.jing.dht` and `dao.jing.remote` (closed store,\n   265\tunreachable peer, malformed envelope) surface unchanged; the linker neither\n   266\tmasks nor re-labels them. A print divergence between hosts (section 8)\n   267\tfails closed as `:absent` or `:address-mismatch`, never as a wrong program.\n   268\t\n   269\t## 5. Format records\n   270\t\n   271\t### 5.1 Stack format (H), contributed by B6\n   272\t\n   273\t    {:format        :yin.debruijn.code\n   274\t     :hash-fn       yin.vm.debruijn-code/image-hash\n   275\t     :validate-fn   yin.vm.debruijn-code/image-defect\n   276\t     :free-names-fn <scan of :load-free operands of the stack vector>}\n   277\t\n   278\tThe stored value is the canonical `:yin.debruijn.code/*` instruction\n   279\tvector. `image-defect` is B1's validator: structural rules, saturation,\n   280\ttarget bounds, terminators, scope by body arity chain, and host scalar\n   281\tsupport. The lift to `:yin.code/*` is B2's `yin.vm.debruijn-linearize/lift`\n   282\tand is how a fetched stack image is executed on the semantic VM in tests.\n   283\t\n   284\t### 5.2 Register format (R), contributed by R5\n   285\t\n   286\t    {:format        :yin.debruijn.register\n   287\t     :hash-fn       yin.vm.debruijn-register-code/register-hash\n   288\t     :validate-fn   yin.vm.debruijn-register-code/register-image-defect\n   289\t     :free-names-fn <scan of :load-free operands across all bodies>}\n   290\t\n   291\tThe stored value is the register image map, `{:bodies ... :instructions\n   292\t...}`, at its own `segment-key`. `register-image-defect` is R1's and R2's\n   293\tvalidator: shape, targets, body scope, register bounds, and the section 4.5\n   294\tlive-set shape, bounds, tail, and exactness rules. The lift to `:yin.code/*`\n   295\tis `yin.vm.debruijn-register-compile/lift`, so the stack design's rule that\n   296\ta fetched image may run on the semantic VM after lifting holds for register\n   297\timages too. R5 contributes this record and its tests; the fetch function\n   298\titself is B6's and R5 must not change it.\n   299\t\n   300\tThe record shape is what makes the function total over formats: adding a\n   301\tthird executable dimension later means adding a third record, not a third\n   302\tlinker.\n   303\t\n   304\t## 6. The H index and the R index\n   305\t\n   306\tThe index maps an identity to a Jing address:\n   307\t\n   308\t    H index:  H -> address   as a datom  [H :yin.debruijn.code/address a]\n   309\t    R index:  R -> address   as a datom  [R :yin.debruijn.register/address a]\n   310\t\n   311\tThe index is composition data, never Jing's: Jing has no roots and no\n   312\tnames by design (stack design D16). The linker takes it as an argument,\n   313\teither a plain map or a set of datoms read through the composition's own\n   314\tstore. The composition holds it as an explicit value; no global registry is\n   315\tintroduced. Whoever publishes an image records its pairing.\n   316\t\n   317\tAn index entry is a claim, not a proof. That is exactly why step 3 exists:\n   318\ta stale or swapped entry is caught as `:hash-mismatch`, and a missing entry\n   319\tis `:absent`. The index is re-minted, not the images, when the storage\n   320\tencoder changes (section 8).\n   321\t\n   322\tB7 later makes the index a name environment with a declared ledger, trust,\n   323\tand provenance, and adds dependency closure for free names. B6 and R5 need\n   324\tonly a value. Until B7, the index's authority is whatever the composition\n   325\tthat holds it decides, and the linker does not judge it.\n   326\t\n   327\t## 7. Same-root pairing (H and R)\n   328\t\n   329\tA host without a register kernel may refuse R and instead resolve, by H, a\n   330\tstack image published for the same named root. The same-root pairing is\n   331\tthe one relation in the linker that no hash checks: H and R have disjoint\n   332\tpreimages, the receiver holds neither the named datoms nor an H-to-R law\n   333\t(the register design's section 1.1 explicitly disclaims one), so a stale\n   334\tor swapped pairing would execute a different program than the R asked for\n   335\twith no diagnostic. The rule is therefore explicit.\n   336\t\n   337\tThe pairing is recorded at mint time beside the named root as datoms:\n   338\t\n   339\t    [root :yin.debruijn.code/hash     H]\n   340\t    [root :yin.debruijn.register/hash R]\n   341\t\n   342\tnever as a bare H-to-R entry. A bare `[H :paired-with R]` fact names no\n   343\tartifact that both hashes are functions of, so nothing could ever check\n   344\tit. Recording both hashes against the root keeps the named datoms, the\n   345\tone lossless source, as the pivot.\n   346\t\n   347\tTwo fallback paths exist, and a receiver must choose one explicitly:\n   348\t\n   349\t1. Trusted fallback. The receiver takes the pairing on composition trust,\n   350\t   the same footing as the H and R indexes themselves. Its ledger,\n   351\t   authority, and provenance are B7's name-environment work. Until B7, a\n   352\t   receiver that follows this path is executing under that trust and must\n   353\t   say so in its outcome.\n   354\t2. Verifying fallback. The receiver does not trust the pairing. It fetches\n   355\t   the named datoms by the root the pairing names, re-lowers them locally\n   356\t   through `adapt` (stack) and `lower-register` (register), and accepts\n   357\t   the fallback only when the recomputed H and R both equal the pair it\n   358\t   was given. Otherwise it refuses with a qualified `:pairing-mismatch`.\n   359\t   That re-lowering is the only check strong enough, because the datoms\n   360\t   are the one artifact both hashes are functions of.\n   361\t\n   362\t## 8. Dependency risk: the transitional content hash\n   363\t\n   364\tOne dependency risk is real and named. `segment-key` hashes the\n   365\torder-normalized print of a value. Every Jing address changes when the\n   366\tDaoJing CBOR encoding lands. H and R do not change, and the linker code\n   367\tdoes not change, but every H index and R index must be re-minted then.\n   368\t\n   369\tConsequences for tests and portability:\n   370\t\n   371\t- Linker tests pin H and R values only. No test ever pins a Jing address\n   372\t  as a golden; an address is always computed in the test from the value.\n   373\t- Until the CBOR encoding lands, Jing addresses are portable only between\n   374\t  hosts whose print of the value agrees. The JVM to Dart transfer corpus\n   375\t  is therefore restricted to print-stable scalars.\n   376\t- A print divergence fails closed: the receiver observes `:absent` (no\n   377\t  such address on its side) or `:address-mismatch` (the value it received\n   378\t  does not print to the address it was asked for), never a wrong program.\n   379\t  Steps 2 and 3 together guarantee the failure mode is refusal.\n   380\t\n   381\t## 9. Stream topology and parked requests\n   382\t\n   383\tReference-by-hash is a stream process, not hidden VM machinery. The linker\n   384\tconsumes executable images and a value-held index, uses only H or R as\n   385\tidentity and request key, emits verified image requests, consumes image\n   386\tvalues, and emits a linked image or an explicit refusal. The boundary is\n   387\t`dao.stream`; local and remote resolution are the same transport-neutral\n   388\tmechanism, and their physical placement is not a linker decision. The\n   389\tcomposition supplies the Jing handle and the index; the linker requires no\n   390\tpersistent publication or discovery.\n   391\t\n   392\tA linked dimension may contain `:call-hash H` once B7 emits it. If H is\n   393\tunavailable, the linked interpreter parks its explicit continuation and\n   394\temits a request value carrying H. The composition resolves H through its\n   395\tindex and runs the fetch function, or reports a qualified absence or\n   396\tunsupported outcome. The request token, parked continuation, and response\n   397\tare data; no callback is retained; gaps, timeouts, and permanent absence\n   398\tremain explicit stream events whose policy belongs to the composition, not\n   399\tthe stream and not the linker. B6 and R5 tests use an explicit fetch call;\n   400\t`:call-hash` is emitted only by B7.\n   401\t\n   402\tCross-host transport of a parked continuation is same-model only until a\n   403\tcross-model continuation format exists (register design section 5.1): a\n   404\tregister continuation travels to a host with a register kernel, a stack\n   405\tcontinuation to a host with the stack VM, and each transfer also needs the\n   406\timage by R or H through this linker.\n   407\t\n   408\t## 10. File boxes\n   409\t\n   410\t### 10.1 B6: stack format and the shared function\n   411\t\n   412\t    New: src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_linker.cljc\n   413\t    New: test/yin/vm/debruijn_linker_test.cljc\n   414\t    Existing edits: none\n   415\t    Depends on: dao.jing (segment-key, segment-matches?, materialize!,\n   416\t    get), dao.jing.dht (create-content-dht, IDhtNet), dao.jing.remote\n   417\t    (content-client, default-handlers, the DaoStream transport), B1\n   418\t    (image-hash, image-defect, descriptor), B2 (lift)\n   419\t    Must not change: merged projection namespace, image-hash, dao.stream,\n   420\t    dao.jing, dao.jing.dht, dao.jing.remote, named VM semantics\n   421\t\n   422\t`yin.vm.debruijn-linker` owns the format record shape, the fetch function,\n   423\tthe index accessors, the refusal vocabulary, and the stack format record.\n   424\t\n   425\t### 10.2 R5: register format record\n   426\t\n   427\t    New: src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_register_linker.cljc\n   428\t    New: test/yin/vm/debruijn_register_linker_test.cljc\n   429\t    Existing edits: none\n   430\t    Depends on: B6's shared linker (yin.vm.debruijn-linker), dao.jing,\n   431\t    dao.jing.dht, dao.jing.remote, R1 (register-hash, register-image-defect,\n   432\t    descriptor, lift)\n   433\t    Must not change: B6's linker function, stack H, dao.jing, dao.jing.dht\n   434\t\n   435\t`yin.vm.debruijn-register-linker` owns the register format record, the R\n   436\tindex accessors, the same-root pairing datoms and their two fallback paths,\n   437\tand tests. Everything else is B6's function and Jing's guarantees.\n   438\t\n   439\tDependency order: B6 depends on B1, B2, and the lift, not on B3 or B4. R5\n   440\tdepends on R1 and on B6's shared function, not on B4, R2, or R4. The two\n   441\tare built together as one unit and may land before B4, R2, or R4.\n   442\t\n   443\t## 11. Completion criteria\n   444\t\n   445\tThe list below merges the B6 list from the stack design and the R5 list\n   446\tfrom the register design into one checklist. Every item marked (H, R) is\n   447\texercised once per format; items marked (R) are register-only; items\n   448\tmarked (shared) are exercised once against the shared function.\n   449\t\n   450\tCross-host transfer:\n   451\t\n   452\t1. (H, R) JVM to Dart transfer over `dao.jing.remote`'s DaoStream\n   453\t   transport, where the receiver initially knows only the identity and an\n   454\t   index. This includes the Dart-side client harness for that path.\n   455\t2. (H, R) Equal normalized results, under the B0 normalizer, when the\n   456\t   fetched image is executed through the existing semantic VM via the\n   457\t   format's lift, compared to local execution.\n   458\t3. (H, R) The cross-host corpus is restricted to print-stable scalars\n   459\t   until the CBOR encoding lands; no golden pins a Jing address.\n   460\t\n   461\tRefusals, each exercised with a deliberately wrong payload or index entry:\n   462\t\n   463\t4. (H, R) `:absent` for an identity the index cannot resolve.\n   464\t5. (H, R) `:address-mismatch` when the received value does not hash to\n   465\t   its address, rejected before load on both a `create-content-dht` handle\n   466\t   and a `content-client` handle.\n   467\t6. (H, R) `:hash-mismatch` when a well-stored value at the indexed address\n   468\t   is not the program asked for, including a descriptor or\n   469\t   contract-version disagreement.\n   470\t7. (H, R) A validator defect in a fetched image refused before load.\n   471\t8. (R) A live-set defect in a fetched register image refused by the\n   472\t   validator before load.\n   473\t9. (H, R) `:unresolved-free` for a `:load-free` name the receiver cannot\n   474\t   resolve through primitives or modules.\n   475\t10. (H, R) `:shadowed-free` for a `:load-free` name the receiver's free\n   476\t    environment or store shadows.\n   477\t11. (shared) A peer serving the wrong content for an address is rejected\n   478\t    before load, on both handle kinds, and the outcome is the linker's\n   479\t    own, not dependent on the handle's verification.\n   480\t\n   481\tSame-root pairing (register design, section 7 here):\n   482\t\n   483\t12. (R) A trusted fallback from R to H that names its trust in the\n   484\t    outcome.\n   485\t13. (R) A verifying fallback that re-lowers the named datoms and refuses a\n   486\t    swapped pairing with `:pairing-mismatch`, and accepts an honest one.\n   487\t\n   488\tStructure:\n   489\t\n   490\t14. (shared) One fetch function serves both records; no format-specific\n   491\t    branch exists inside the function, only calls through the record.\n   492\t15. (H, R) Tests use an explicit fetch call; no `:call-hash` instruction\n   493\t    is emitted or consumed.\n   494\t16. (shared) No global loader, registry, callback, or cache is introduced;\n   495\t    the handle and the index are arguments.\n   496\t\n   497\tVerification runs on JVM, Node/CLJS, and ClojureDart, plus kondo, cljstyle,\n   498\tand the 80-column design check.\n   499\t\n   500\t## 12. Non-goals\n   501\t\n   502\tThe linker does not:\n   503\t\n   504\t- Add a transport, a peer lookup, a cache, or a responder. All four are\n   505\t  `dao.jing`'s and `dao.jing.remote`'s.\n   506\t- Add a JIT, equality saturation, or any change to the merged dormant\n   507\t  projection or its fingerprint.\n   508\t- Emit or interpret `:call-hash`. That is B7's dependency closure linker,\n   509\t  along with strongly connected component manifests, hash-of-unit\n   510\t  closure, and the retry, timeout, and permanent-absence event\n   511\t  vocabulary.\n   512\t- Supply a name environment with ledger, trust, or provenance. That is\n   513\t  B7. B6 and R5 take the index as a value.\n   514\t- Translate between H and the Jing address, or between H and R. Neither\n   515\t  translation exists; the index is a recorded pairing and the same-root\n   516\t  pairing is a recorded composition fact.\n   517\t- Provide a cross-model continuation format. Parked continuations travel\n   518\t  same-model only.\n   519\t- Change `dao.stream`, `dao.jing`, `dao.jing.dht`, `dao.jing.remote`,\n   520\t  `image-hash`, `register-hash`, or named VM semantics.\n   521\t\n   522\t## 13. Decisions inherited\n   523\t\n   524\tThe stack design's D6, D8, D9, D11, D12, D13, D14, D15, and D16 govern this\n   525\tdocument and are not restated. The register design's DECIDED 2 (H and R\n   526\tare peer identities with disjoint preimages) and its DEFERRED entry for the\n   527\tB7 ledger that the same-root pairing's trust rests on apply as written.\n   528\tStill deferred, inherited from the stack design: the request/response\n   529\tschema and contract stamps for parked requests, and the exact retry,\n   530\ttimeout, and permanent-absence event vocabulary.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
