{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.linker.md | sed -n '304,540p'\nnl -ba src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc | sed -n '309,545p'\"","aggregated_output":"   304\t## 6. The H index and the R index\n   305\t\n   306\tThe index maps an identity to a Jing address:\n   307\t\n   308\t    H index:  H -> address   as a datom  [H :yin.debruijn.code/address a]\n   309\t    R index:  R -> address   as a datom  [R :yin.debruijn.register/address a]\n   310\t\n   311\tThe index is composition data, never Jing's: Jing has no roots and no\n   312\tnames by design (stack design D16). The linker takes it as an argument,\n   313\teither a plain map or a set of datoms read through the composition's own\n   314\tstore. The composition holds it as an explicit value; no global registry is\n   315\tintroduced. Whoever publishes an image records its pairing.\n   316\t\n   317\tAn index entry is a claim, not a proof. That is exactly why step 3 exists:\n   318\ta stale or swapped entry is caught as `:hash-mismatch`, and a missing entry\n   319\tis `:absent`. The index is re-minted, not the images, when the storage\n   320\tencoder changes (section 8).\n   321\t\n   322\tB7 later makes the index a name environment with a declared ledger, trust,\n   323\tand provenance, and adds dependency closure for free names. B6 and R5 need\n   324\tonly a value. Until B7, the index's authority is whatever the composition\n   325\tthat holds it decides, and the linker does not judge it.\n   326\t\n   327\t## 7. Same-root pairing (H and R)\n   328\t\n   329\tA host without a register kernel may refuse R and instead resolve, by H, a\n   330\tstack image published for the same named root. The same-root pairing is\n   331\tthe one relation in the linker that no hash checks: H and R have disjoint\n   332\tpreimages, the receiver holds neither the named datoms nor an H-to-R law\n   333\t(the register design's section 1.1 explicitly disclaims one), so a stale\n   334\tor swapped pairing would execute a different program than the R asked for\n   335\twith no diagnostic. The rule is therefore explicit.\n   336\t\n   337\tThe pairing is recorded at mint time beside the named root as datoms:\n   338\t\n   339\t    [root :yin.debruijn.code/hash     H]\n   340\t    [root :yin.debruijn.register/hash R]\n   341\t\n   342\tnever as a bare H-to-R entry. A bare `[H :paired-with R]` fact names no\n   343\tartifact that both hashes are functions of, so nothing could ever check\n   344\tit. Recording both hashes against the root keeps the named datoms, the\n   345\tone lossless source, as the pivot.\n   346\t\n   347\tTwo fallback paths exist, and a receiver must choose one explicitly:\n   348\t\n   349\t1. Trusted fallback. The receiver takes the pairing on composition trust,\n   350\t   the same footing as the H and R indexes themselves. Its ledger,\n   351\t   authority, and provenance are B7's name-environment work. Until B7, a\n   352\t   receiver that follows this path is executing under that trust and must\n   353\t   say so in its outcome.\n   354\t2. Verifying fallback. The receiver does not trust the pairing. It fetches\n   355\t   the named datoms by the root the pairing names, re-lowers them locally\n   356\t   through `adapt` (stack) and `lower-register` (register), and accepts\n   357\t   the fallback only when the recomputed H and R both equal the pair it\n   358\t   was given. Otherwise it refuses with a qualified `:pairing-mismatch`.\n   359\t   That re-lowering is the only check strong enough, because the datoms\n   360\t   are the one artifact both hashes are functions of.\n   361\t\n   362\t## 8. Dependency risk: the transitional content hash\n   363\t\n   364\tOne dependency risk is real and named. `segment-key` hashes the\n   365\torder-normalized print of a value. Every Jing address changes when the\n   366\tDaoJing CBOR encoding lands. H and R do not change, and the linker code\n   367\tdoes not change, but every H index and R index must be re-minted then.\n   368\t\n   369\tConsequences for tests and portability:\n   370\t\n   371\t- Linker tests pin H and R values only. No test ever pins a Jing address\n   372\t  as a golden; an address is always computed in the test from the value.\n   373\t- Until the CBOR encoding lands, Jing addresses are portable only between\n   374\t  hosts whose print of the value agrees. The JVM to Dart transfer corpus\n   375\t  is therefore restricted to print-stable scalars.\n   376\t- A print divergence fails closed: the receiver observes `:absent` (no\n   377\t  such address on its side) or `:address-mismatch` (the value it received\n   378\t  does not print to the address it was asked for), never a wrong program.\n   379\t  Steps 2 and 3 together guarantee the failure mode is refusal.\n   380\t\n   381\t## 9. Stream topology and parked requests\n   382\t\n   383\tReference-by-hash is a stream process, not hidden VM machinery. The linker\n   384\tconsumes executable images and a value-held index, uses only H or R as\n   385\tidentity and request key, emits verified image requests, consumes image\n   386\tvalues, and emits a linked image or an explicit refusal. The boundary is\n   387\t`dao.stream`; local and remote resolution are the same transport-neutral\n   388\tmechanism, and their physical placement is not a linker decision. The\n   389\tcomposition supplies the Jing handle and the index; the linker requires no\n   390\tpersistent publication or discovery.\n   391\t\n   392\tA linked dimension may contain `:call-hash H` once B7 emits it. If H is\n   393\tunavailable, the linked interpreter parks its explicit continuation and\n   394\temits a request value carrying H. The composition resolves H through its\n   395\tindex and runs the fetch function, or reports a qualified absence or\n   396\tunsupported outcome. The request token, parked continuation, and response\n   397\tare data; no callback is retained; gaps, timeouts, and permanent absence\n   398\tremain explicit stream events whose policy belongs to the composition, not\n   399\tthe stream and not the linker. B6 and R5 tests use an explicit fetch call;\n   400\t`:call-hash` is emitted only by B7.\n   401\t\n   402\tCross-host transport of a parked continuation is same-model only until a\n   403\tcross-model continuation format exists (register design section 5.1): a\n   404\tregister continuation travels to a host with a register kernel, a stack\n   405\tcontinuation to a host with the stack VM, and each transfer also needs the\n   406\timage by R or H through this linker.\n   407\t\n   408\t## 10. File boxes\n   409\t\n   410\t### 10.1 B6: stack format and the shared function\n   411\t\n   412\t    New: src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_linker.cljc\n   413\t    New: test/yin/vm/debruijn_linker_test.cljc\n   414\t    Existing edits: none\n   415\t    Depends on: dao.jing (segment-key, segment-matches?, materialize!,\n   416\t    get), dao.jing.dht (create-content-dht, IDhtNet), dao.jing.remote\n   417\t    (content-client, default-handlers, the DaoStream transport), B1\n   418\t    (image-hash, image-defect, descriptor), B2 (lift)\n   419\t    Must not change: merged projection namespace, image-hash, dao.stream,\n   420\t    dao.jing, dao.jing.dht, dao.jing.remote, named VM semantics\n   421\t\n   422\t`yin.vm.debruijn-linker` owns the format record shape, the fetch function,\n   423\tthe index accessors, the refusal vocabulary, and the stack format record.\n   424\t\n   425\t### 10.2 R5: register format record\n   426\t\n   427\t    New: src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_register_linker.cljc\n   428\t    New: test/yin/vm/debruijn_register_linker_test.cljc\n   429\t    Existing edits: none\n   430\t    Depends on: B6's shared linker (yin.vm.debruijn-linker), dao.jing,\n   431\t    dao.jing.dht, dao.jing.remote, R1 (register-hash, register-image-defect,\n   432\t    descriptor, lift)\n   433\t    Must not change: B6's linker function, stack H, dao.jing, dao.jing.dht\n   434\t\n   435\t`yin.vm.debruijn-register-linker` owns the register format record, the R\n   436\tindex accessors, the same-root pairing datoms and their two fallback paths,\n   437\tand tests. Everything else is B6's function and Jing's guarantees.\n   438\t\n   439\tDependency order: B6 depends on B1, B2, and the lift, not on B3 or B4. R5\n   440\tdepends on R1 and on B6's shared function, not on B4, R2, or R4. The two\n   441\tare built together as one unit and may land before B4, R2, or R4.\n   442\t\n   443\t## 11. Completion criteria\n   444\t\n   445\tThe list below merges the B6 list from the stack design and the R5 list\n   446\tfrom the register design into one checklist. Every item marked (H, R) is\n   447\texercised once per format; items marked (R) are register-only; items\n   448\tmarked (shared) are exercised once against the shared function.\n   449\t\n   450\tCross-host transfer:\n   451\t\n   452\t1. (H, R) JVM to Dart transfer over `dao.jing.remote`'s DaoStream\n   453\t   transport, where the receiver initially knows only the identity and an\n   454\t   index. This includes the Dart-side client harness for that path.\n   455\t2. (H, R) Equal normalized results, under the B0 normalizer, when the\n   456\t   fetched image is executed through the existing semantic VM via the\n   457\t   format's lift, compared to local execution.\n   458\t3. (H, R) The cross-host corpus is restricted to print-stable scalars\n   459\t   until the CBOR encoding lands; no golden pins a Jing address.\n   460\t\n   461\tRefusals, each exercised with a deliberately wrong payload or index entry:\n   462\t\n   463\t4. (H, R) `:absent` for an identity the index cannot resolve.\n   464\t5. (H, R) `:address-mismatch` when the received value does not hash to\n   465\t   its address, rejected before load on both a `create-content-dht` handle\n   466\t   and a `content-client` handle.\n   467\t6. (H, R) `:hash-mismatch` when a well-stored value at the indexed address\n   468\t   is not the program asked for, including a descriptor or\n   469\t   contract-version disagreement.\n   470\t7. (H, R) A validator defect in a fetched image refused before load.\n   471\t8. (R) A live-set defect in a fetched register image refused by the\n   472\t   validator before load.\n   473\t9. (H, R) `:unresolved-free` for a `:load-free` name the receiver cannot\n   474\t   resolve through primitives or modules.\n   475\t10. (H, R) `:shadowed-free` for a `:load-free` name the receiver's free\n   476\t    environment or store shadows.\n   477\t11. (shared) A peer serving the wrong content for an address is rejected\n   478\t    before load, on both handle kinds, and the outcome is the linker's\n   479\t    own, not dependent on the handle's verification.\n   480\t\n   481\tSame-root pairing (register design, section 7 here):\n   482\t\n   483\t12. (R) A trusted fallback from R to H that names its trust in the\n   484\t    outcome.\n   485\t13. (R) A verifying fallback that re-lowers the named datoms and refuses a\n   486\t    swapped pairing with `:pairing-mismatch`, and accepts an honest one.\n   487\t\n   488\tStructure:\n   489\t\n   490\t14. (shared) One fetch function serves both records; no format-specific\n   491\t    branch exists inside the function, only calls through the record.\n   492\t15. (H, R) Tests use an explicit fetch call; no `:call-hash` instruction\n   493\t    is emitted or consumed.\n   494\t16. (shared) No global loader, registry, callback, or cache is introduced;\n   495\t    the handle and the index are arguments.\n   496\t\n   497\tVerification runs on JVM, Node/CLJS, and ClojureDart, plus kondo, cljstyle,\n   498\tand the 80-column design check.\n   499\t\n   500\t## 12. Non-goals\n   501\t\n   502\tThe linker does not:\n   503\t\n   504\t- Add a transport, a peer lookup, a cache, or a responder. All four are\n   505\t  `dao.jing`'s and `dao.jing.remote`'s.\n   506\t- Add a JIT, equality saturation, or any change to the merged dormant\n   507\t  projection or its fingerprint.\n   508\t- Emit or interpret `:call-hash`. That is B7's dependency closure linker,\n   509\t  along with strongly connected component manifests, hash-of-unit\n   510\t  closure, and the retry, timeout, and permanent-absence event\n   511\t  vocabulary.\n   512\t- Supply a name environment with ledger, trust, or provenance. That is\n   513\t  B7. B6 and R5 take the index as a value.\n   514\t- Translate between H and the Jing address, or between H and R. Neither\n   515\t  translation exists; the index is a recorded pairing and the same-root\n   516\t  pairing is a recorded composition fact.\n   517\t- Provide a cross-model continuation format. Parked continuations travel\n   518\t  same-model only.\n   519\t- Change `dao.stream`, `dao.jing`, `dao.jing.dht`, `dao.jing.remote`,\n   520\t  `image-hash`, `register-hash`, or named VM semantics.\n   521\t\n   522\t## 13. Decisions inherited\n   523\t\n   524\tThe stack design's D6, D8, D9, D11, D12, D13, D14, D15, and D16 govern this\n   525\tdocument and are not restated. The register design's DECIDED 2 (H and R\n   526\tare peer identities with disjoint preimages) and its DEFERRED entry for the\n   527\tB7 ledger that the same-root pairing's trust rests on apply as written.\n   528\tStill deferred, inherited from the stack design: the request/response\n   529\tschema and contract stamps for parked requests, and the exact retry,\n   530\ttimeout, and permanent-absence event vocabulary.\n   309\t(def default-hash-algorithm\n   310\t  \"The default algorithm used for implicit minting (content-hash, segment-key,\n   311\t   materialize!). Never consulted when verifying an existing address.\"\n   312\t  :blake3)\n   313\t\n   314\t\n   315\t(def registry\n   316\t  \"The closed, immutable algorithm registry. Both initial entries produce\n   317\t   32-byte (64 lowercase hex character) digests.\"\n   318\t  {:blake3 {:address-id \"blake3\", :digest-bytes 32}\n   319\t   :sha256 {:address-id \"sha256\", :digest-bytes 32}})\n   320\t\n   321\t\n   322\t(def ^:private registry-by-address-id\n   323\t  (into {} (map (fn [[k v]] [(:address-id v) k])) registry))\n   324\t\n   325\t\n   326\t(def ^:private algorithm-id-pattern\n   327\t  \"Algorithm address identifiers are lowercase ASCII alphanumeric strings.\"\n   328\t  #\"[a-z0-9]+\")\n   329\t\n   330\t\n   331\t(def ^:private hex-digit-pattern\n   332\t  #\"[0-9a-f]+\")\n   333\t\n   334\t\n   335\t(defn blake3-bytes\n   336\t  \"BLAKE3 lowercase hex digest of host bytes bs (byte[] on the JVM,\n   337\t   Uint8Array on ClojureScript, Uint8List on Dart).\"\n   338\t  [bs]\n   339\t  #?(:clj (let [b (io.github.rctcwyvrn.blake3.Blake3/newInstance)]\n   340\t            (.update b ^bytes bs)\n   341\t            (.hexdigest b))\n   342\t     :cljs (noble-utils/bytesToHex (noble-blake3/blake3 bs))\n   343\t     :cljd (let [^Uint8List u8 (if (instance? Uint8List bs)\n   344\t                                 bs\n   345\t                                 (Uint8List.fromList bs))]\n   346\t             (blake3-dart/blake3Hex u8))))\n   347\t\n   348\t\n   349\t(defn blake3\n   350\t  \"BLAKE3 lowercase hex digest of the UTF-8 bytes of string s.\"\n   351\t  [s]\n   352\t  #?(:clj (let [b (io.github.rctcwyvrn.blake3.Blake3/newInstance)]\n   353\t            (.update b (.getBytes ^String s \"UTF-8\"))\n   354\t            (.hexdigest b))\n   355\t     :cljs (blake3-bytes (noble-utils/utf8ToBytes s))\n   356\t     :cljd (blake3-bytes (convert/utf8.encode s))))\n   357\t\n   358\t\n   359\t(defn digest-bytes\n   360\t  \"Digest host bytes bs with the specified algorithm keyword (:blake3, :sha256).\n   361\t   Returns lowercase hexadecimal string. Unknown algorithms throw.\"\n   362\t  [algorithm bs]\n   363\t  (case algorithm\n   364\t    :blake3 (blake3-bytes bs)\n   365\t    :sha256 (sha256-bytes bs)\n   366\t    (throw (ex-info (str \"unsupported hash algorithm: \" algorithm)\n   367\t                    {:algorithm algorithm}))))\n   368\t\n   369\t\n   370\t(defn digest-string\n   371\t  \"Digest string s as UTF-8 bytes with the specified algorithm keyword.\n   372\t   Returns lowercase hexadecimal string. Unknown algorithms throw.\"\n   373\t  [algorithm s]\n   374\t  (case algorithm\n   375\t    :blake3 (blake3 s)\n   376\t    :sha256 (sha256 s)\n   377\t    (throw (ex-info (str \"unsupported hash algorithm: \" algorithm)\n   378\t                    {:algorithm algorithm}))))\n   379\t\n   380\t\n   381\t(defn canonical-bytes\n   382\t  \"Host UTF-8 bytes (byte[] / Uint8Array / Uint8List) of the\n   383\t   order-normalized print of v -- the exact bytes content hashing digests, so\n   384\t   `(digest-bytes algo (canonical-bytes v))` is\n   385\t   `(content-hash v {:algorithm algo})`.\n   386\t   This is the byte form Jing content travels in across a `dao.stream`\n   387\t   boundary.\"\n   388\t  [v]\n   389\t  (let [text (order-normalized-print v)]\n   390\t    #?(:clj (.getBytes ^String text \"UTF-8\")\n   391\t       :cljs (js/Uint8Array.from (crypt/stringToUtf8ByteArray text))\n   392\t       :cljd (utf8-bytes text))))\n   393\t\n   394\t\n   395\t(defn content-hash\n   396\t  \"Hash the canonical-bytes of v using the specified algorithm (defaulting to\n   397\t   default-hash-algorithm, i.e. :blake3). Returns lowercase hexadecimal string.\n   398\t   Arities: [v] and [v {:keys [algorithm]}].\"\n   399\t  ([v]\n   400\t   (content-hash v {:algorithm default-hash-algorithm}))\n   401\t  ([v opts]\n   402\t   (let [algo (clojure.core/get opts :algorithm default-hash-algorithm)]\n   403\t     (digest-bytes algo (canonical-bytes v)))))\n   404\t\n   405\t\n   406\t(defn segment-key\n   407\t  \"Mint the content-addressed key for an opaque payload, derived solely from\n   408\t   the payload: :segment/<algorithm-id>-<digest>.\n   409\t   The 1-arg form defaults to default-hash-algorithm (:blake3).\n   410\t   The 2-arg form accepts {:algorithm :sha256} (or any registered algorithm).\"\n   411\t  ([v]\n   412\t   (segment-key v {:algorithm default-hash-algorithm}))\n   413\t  ([v opts]\n   414\t   (let [algo (clojure.core/get opts :algorithm default-hash-algorithm)\n   415\t         reg (clojure.core/get registry algo)]\n   416\t     (when-not reg\n   417\t       (throw (ex-info (str \"unsupported hash algorithm: \" algo)\n   418\t                       {:algorithm algo})))\n   419\t     (let [algo-id (:address-id reg)\n   420\t           digest (content-hash v {:algorithm algo})]\n   421\t       (keyword \"segment\" (str algo-id \"-\" digest))))))\n   422\t\n   423\t\n   424\t(defn parse-segment-address\n   425\t  \"The authoritative address parser: requires a `segment` namespace keyword,\n   426\t   splits the name on the first `-` into algorithm identifier and digest,\n   427\t   looks the identifier up by exact match (no prefix matching), validates\n   428\t   digest length from the registry entry and lowercase-hex text, and\n   429\t   reconstructs the canonical address to reject alternative spellings.\n   430\t   Returns {:algorithm :<algo>, :digest \\\"<hex>\\\", :canonical :segment/...}\n   431\t   or nil on any rejection (never throws).\"\n   432\t  [address]\n   433\t  (when (keyword? address)\n   434\t    (when (= \"segment\" (namespace address))\n   435\t      (let [n (name address)\n   436\t            i (str/index-of n \"-\")]\n   437\t        (when (and i (pos? i))\n   438\t          (let [algo-id (subs n 0 i)\n   439\t                digest (subs n (inc i))]\n   440\t            (when (re-matches algorithm-id-pattern algo-id)\n   441\t              (when-let [algo (clojure.core/get registry-by-address-id algo-id)]\n   442\t                (let [{:keys [digest-bytes]}\n   443\t                      (clojure.core/get registry algo)]\n   444\t                  (when (and (= (* 2 digest-bytes) (count digest))\n   445\t                             (re-matches hex-digit-pattern digest))\n   446\t                    (let [canonical (keyword \"segment\"\n   447\t                                             (str algo-id \"-\" digest))]\n   448\t                      (when (= canonical address)\n   449\t                        {:algorithm algo,\n   450\t                         :digest digest,\n   451\t                         :canonical canonical}))))))))))))\n   452\t\n   453\t\n   454\t(defn segment-address?\n   455\t  \"True when x is a valid content address in the closed registry:\n   456\t   :segment/<algorithm>-<digest-hex>.\"\n   457\t  [x]\n   458\t  (boolean (parse-segment-address x)))\n   459\t\n   460\t\n   461\t(defn segment-algorithm\n   462\t  \"The algorithm keyword (:blake3, :sha256) carried by a segment address.\n   463\t   Throws on invalid input.\"\n   464\t  [address]\n   465\t  (if-let [parsed (parse-segment-address address)]\n   466\t    (:algorithm parsed)\n   467\t    (throw (ex-info \"not a segment content address\" {:address address}))))\n   468\t\n   469\t\n   470\t(defn segment-digest\n   471\t  \"The lowercase hex digest carried by a segment address.\n   472\t   Throws on invalid input.\"\n   473\t  [address]\n   474\t  (if-let [parsed (parse-segment-address address)]\n   475\t    (:digest parsed)\n   476\t    (throw (ex-info \"not a segment content address\" {:address address}))))\n   477\t\n   478\t\n   479\t(defn segment-hash\n   480\t  \"The content hash carried by a segment address. Equivalent to segment-digest.\n   481\t   Total only over valid segment addresses; throws on invalid input.\"\n   482\t  [k]\n   483\t  (segment-digest k))\n   484\t\n   485\t\n   486\t(defn segment-matches?\n   487\t  \"Total verification predicate: true when address is a valid segment address\n   488\t   and its digest matches the hash of canonical-bytes of payload under the\n   489\t   address-carried algorithm.\n   490\t   Never throws: malformed addresses, unknown algorithms, digest mismatches,\n   491\t   and canonical-encoder refusal all return false.\"\n   492\t  [address payload]\n   493\t  (if-let [{:keys [algorithm digest]} (parse-segment-address address)]\n   494\t    (try\n   495\t      (let [bs (canonical-bytes payload)\n   496\t            actual (digest-bytes algorithm bs)]\n   497\t        (= digest actual))\n   498\t      (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) _\n   499\t        false))\n   500\t    false))\n   501\t\n   502\t\n   503\t(defn materialize!\n   504\t  \"Content-address payload and store it through the handle's backend.\n   505\t\n   506\t   Supports [handle payload] (defaulting to default-hash-algorithm, :blake3)\n   507\t   and [handle payload {:keys [algorithm]}].\n   508\t\n   509\t   The address is derived solely from the payload via segment-key, and the\n   510\t   backend effect :put-content-fn is invoked as (put-content-fn address\n   511\t   payload). The backend must answer with one of:\n   512\t\n   513\t     :inserted — the value is durably stored now;\n   514\t     :present  — an equal value is already stored under that address.\n   515\t\n   516\t   Any other answer is an invalid backend result and throws: DaoJing does\n   517\t   not accept ambiguous truthiness. The address is returned only after the\n   518\t   backend reports success.\n   519\t\n   520\t   On :present the stored value is read back through :get-content-fn and\n   521\t   verified to hash to the address with segment-matches?. Equal content is\n   522\t   idempotent and returns the same address. Content that does not hash to its\n   523\t   own address is an integrity failure and throws loudly. Nothing is ever\n   524\t   overwritten.\"\n   525\t  ([handle payload]\n   526\t   (materialize! handle payload {:algorithm default-hash-algorithm}))\n   527\t  ([handle payload opts]\n   528\t   (let [put (:put-content-fn handle)\n   529\t         get-fn (:get-content-fn handle)]\n   530\t     (when-not (fn? put)\n   531\t       (throw (ex-info \"dao.jing handle requires :put-content-fn\"\n   532\t                       {:handle handle})))\n   533\t     (when-not (fn? get-fn)\n   534\t       (throw (ex-info \"dao.jing handle requires :get-content-fn\"\n   535\t                       {:handle handle})))\n   536\t     (let [address (segment-key payload opts)\n   537\t           result (put address payload)]\n   538\t       (case result\n   539\t         :inserted address\n   540\t         :present\n   541\t         (let [stored (get-fn address content-missing)]\n   542\t           (cond\n   543\t             (identical? stored content-missing)\n   544\t             (throw\n   545\t               (ex-info\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"agent_message","text":"The BLAKE3 wording is accurate, and the address/H distinction is preserved. The fetch pseudocode still calls one-argument `segment-key`, though, which uses today’s default rather than the algorithm carried by the address. I’m checking the remaining extraction for similar inconsistencies.\n"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'segment-matches|mismatch|throw|fetch-vector|defn image-hash|defn register-hash|hash-algorithm|digest-bytes|sha256' src/cljc/dao/jing/dht.cljc src/cljc/dao/jing/remote.cljc src/cljc/yin/vm/content.cljc src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_code.cljc src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_register_code.cljc\nrg -n -i 'sha.?256|blake3|segment-key' docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.linker.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
