{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"git show HEAD:docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md | sed -n '1000,1100p'\nnl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md | sed -n '772,919p'\nnl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md | sed -n '100,125p;267,300p;1040,1111p'\"","aggregated_output":"2026-09-24 01:58:41.862 xcodebuild[67544:21945777]  DVTFilePathFSEvents: Failed to start fs event stream.\n2026-09-24 01:58:42.116 xcodebuild[67544:21945774] [MT] DVTDeveloperPaths: Failed to get length of DARWIN_USER_CACHE_DIR from confstr(3), error = Error Domain=NSPOSIXErrorDomain Code=5 \"Input/output error\". Using NSCachesDirectory instead.\ngit: warning: confstr() failed with code 5: couldn't get path of DARWIN_USER_TEMP_DIR; using /tmp instead\ngit: error: couldn't create cache file '/tmp/xcrun_db-8yEv8xcm' (errno=Operation not permitted)\n2026-09-24 01:58:42.850 xcodebuild[67551:21945821]  DVTFilePathFSEvents: Failed to start fs event stream.\n2026-09-24 01:58:43.071 xcodebuild[67551:21945819] [MT] DVTDeveloperPaths: Failed to get length of DARWIN_USER_CACHE_DIR from confstr(3), error = Error Domain=NSPOSIXErrorDomain Code=5 \"Input/output error\". Using NSCachesDirectory instead.\nconventions the kernel must share. Its completion requires parity for\nvalues, errors, effects, stores, stream outcomes, and blocked states, and\ndeterministic stream/effect behavior. The pure-program tier may merge\nbefore R2 lands; R4 is complete only when both tiers are.\n\nThe phase order runs on two parallel tracks after R1: on the register\ntrack, R2's descriptor and liveness extension and R4's pure-program tier,\nthen R4's effects tier once B4 and R2 exist, then R3 against the real\nkernel; on the linker track, R5 together with B6, depending on R1 and B6\nonly and free to land before R2 or R4.\n\n### R5: linker integration over dao.jing\n\n    New: src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_register_linker.cljc\n    New: test/yin/vm/debruijn_register_linker_test.cljc\n    Existing edits: none\n    Depends on: B6's shared linker (`yin.vm.debruijn-linker`), dao.jing,\n    dao.jing.dht, dao.jing.remote, R1 (register-hash, the validator, the\n    descriptor)\n    Must not change: B6's linker function, stack H, dao.jing, dao.jing.dht\n\nThe linker discipline is `dao.jing` over `dao.stream`, as the stack\ndesign's B6 box now specifies, and it is one function parameterized by a\nformat record. R5 is that function's second format, not a second linker.\nIt contributes the register format record\n`{:format :yin.debruijn.register :hash-fn register-hash :validate-fn ...\n:free-names-fn ...}`, the register image stored in Jing\nas its own value (the `{:bodies :instructions}` map) at its\n`segment-key`, and an R index `[R :yin.debruijn.register/address\naddress]` with the same status as B6's H index: composition data, never\nJing's. R is not the Jing address for the same reason H is not, and R5\npins R values only, never addresses.\n\nFetch follows B6's six steps with R in place of H: the linker verifies\nthe value against its Jing address, verifies `register-hash` against R\n(the descriptor hash and contract version are inside R, so there is no\nseparate descriptor check), runs the register validator including the\nsection 4.5 live-set rules, runs the D11 and D15 closure check over\n`:load-free` operands of the validated image, and returns the verified\nimage. No global loader or callback is introduced.\n\nA host without a register kernel may refuse R and instead resolve, by H,\na stack image published for the same named root. That same-root pairing\nis the one relation in the linker that no hash checks: H and R have\ndisjoint preimages, the receiver holds neither the named datoms nor an\nH-to-R law (section 1.1 disclaims one), so a stale or swapped pairing\nwould execute a different program than the R asked for with no\ndiagnostic. The rule is therefore explicit. The pairing is recorded at\nmint time beside the named root as datoms, `[root\n:yin.debruijn.code/hash H]` and `[root :yin.debruijn.register/hash R]`,\nnever as a bare H-to-R entry. Trusting it is composition trust, on the\nsame footing as the H and R indexes, and its ledger, authority, and\nprovenance are B7's name-environment work; until B7, a receiver that\nfollows the fallback is executing under that trust and must say so in its\noutcome. A receiver that requires verification does not trust the\npairing: it fetches the named datoms by the root the pairing names,\nre-lowers them locally through `adapt` and `lower-register`, and accepts\nthe fallback only when the recomputed H and R both equal the pair it was\ngiven; otherwise it refuses with a qualified `:pairing-mismatch`. That\nre-lowering is the only check strong enough, because the datoms are the\none artifact both hashes are functions of.\n\nNew work is the format record, the R index, the same-root pairing\ndatoms and their verification path, and tests; everything else is B6's\nfunction and Jing's guarantees. R5 depends on R1 and on B6's shared\nfunction, not on B4, R2, or R4, and is built together with B6 as one\nunit, since the shared function's first two formats are best written\nagainst each other. Completion requires the B6 completion list with R in\nplace of H, a live-set defect in a fetched image refused by the validator\nbefore load, and both fallback outcomes: a trusted fallback that names\nits trust, and a verifying fallback that refuses a swapped pairing with\n`:pairing-mismatch`.\n\n## 7. Non-goals and protected surfaces\n\nThis design does not alter any B0-B7 phase's semantics, the stack VM, named\ndatoms, the dormant projection, or the AST-walker. It does not change B1's\nH, its scalar bytes, its validator, or its wire protocol. It does not add\nequality saturation, a JIT, lambda lifting, ANF, global distribution, or a\nnew continuation interchange format. B2's adoption of the resolver is the\ndecided shared upstream recorded in section 2.2.\n\nThe register lowerer consumes the resolved tuples, never the stack image and\nnever named datoms directly. It is not a second stack lowerer: it emits only\n`:yin.debruijn.register/*` and B2 remains the sole producer of\n`:yin.debruijn.code/*`. Its input remains available after the transform.\nRegister code is not portable to a stack-only host; such a host obtains a\nstack image for the same program by H or by lowering the named datoms.\n\nThe resolver is not a second lossless source view. It has no identity, is\nnot persisted, and is not a fetch key; its inverse exists for tests only.\n\n## 8. Risks and decisions\n\nRisks include register-allocation drift, spill policy becoming observable,\neffect ordering, register continuation shape, drift between the stack and\nregister lowerers, and the permanent second-evaluator maintenance surface\naccepted in DECIDED 1.\n\nDECIDED:\n\n   772\t### 7.2 Committed B6 and later B7 linker topology\n   773\t\n   774\tReference-by-hash is a stream process, not hidden VM machinery. B6 is the\n   775\tcommitted closed-image fetch path. It consumes executable images and a\n   776\tvalue-held name environment, uses only H as identity and request key, emits\n   777\tverified image requests, consumes image bytes, and emits a linked image or an\n   778\texplicit refusal. This follows the axiom that\n   779\tall IO and data flow through append-only streams. No global mutable registry\n   780\tis introduced, and no direct function-to-function call crosses the boundary.\n   781\tThe boundary is `dao.stream`; local and remote resolution are the same\n   782\ttransport-neutral mechanism, and their physical placement is not a linker\n   783\tdecision.\n   784\t\n   785\t`dao.jing` stores and fetches the image as a value at its own content\n   786\taddress, over `dao.stream` through `dao.jing.remote`. B verifies the\n   787\treceived value twice, against its Jing address with `segment-key` and\n   788\tagainst H with `image-hash`, and never treats `jing/segment-key` as H. The\n   789\tintegrity rule is the same as hashing wire bytes before trusting them; the\n   790\tmechanics are Jing's, which hash the decoded value, and the B6 box states\n   791\tthem. `load-vector` and `:code-aliases` remain named-VM machinery. B6 does\n   792\tnot require persistent publication or discovery: the composition supplies\n   793\tthe Jing handle and the H index.\n   794\t\n   795\tA linked dimension may contain `:call-hash H` once B7 emits it. If H is\n   796\tunavailable, the linked interpreter parks its explicit continuation and emits a\n   797\tREQUEST value carrying H. The composition resolves H to a Jing address\n   798\tthrough its index and fetches the value, or reports a qualified absence\n   799\tor unsupported outcome. B verifies the value against its address and\n   800\tagainst H, rejects mismatches, validates, runs the receiver closure check,\n   801\tloads only verified images, and resumes. The request token, parked\n   802\tcontinuation, and response are data; no callback is retained. Gaps,\n   803\ttimeouts, and permanent absence remain explicit stream events.\n   804\t\n   805\tB7 supplies dependency closure for free names. Mutually recursive definitions\n   806\tform one strongly connected component, whose members are ordered by canonical\n   807\timage hash before the component hash is computed. A name environment is a\n   808\tvalue or stream with a declared ledger, trust, and provenance. Streams provide\n   809\tfailure events but do not choose retry, timeout, or permanent-absence policy.\n   810\t\n   811\tThis does not require a semantic change to B0 through B5. B3 already makes\n   812\tloading and continuation state explicit, and B4 carries park and resume as\n   813\tdata transitions. The VM accepts loaded images only as values in its state and\n   814\tnever invokes a loader; absence is a park plus a request emission.\n   815\t\n   816\tCompletion for B6 requires closed-image fetch by H, verified cross-runtime\n   817\texecution, malformed or missing-address diagnostics, and explicit absence and\n   818\tunsupported outcomes. B7 additionally requires cycle-safe manifests and\n   819\tdependency closure. Neither modifies the merged dormant projection or\n   820\testablishes any property of the Unison runtime, which is UNVERIFIED here.\n   821\t\n   822\t### 7.3 Lossless source view non-goal\n   823\t\n   824\tThe VM does not build a second lossless record DAG. Named datoms remain the\n   825\tlossless source, and lexical positions, binder diagnostics, and provenance are\n   826\tderived into the executable image as needed. A separate inverse view would\n   827\trequire its own design and is outside B0 through B7.\n   828\t\n   829\tIf a future coarser semantic-deduplication identity is wanted, it must be\n   830\tderived from the executable image by hashing a normalized view with canonical\n   831\tscalars and tail flags dropped. It must not reuse or equal the dormant pinned\n   832\tprojection fingerprint; it would be a new derived value and a separate\n   833\tdecision.\n   834\t\n   835\t## 8. Risks and owner decisions\n   836\t\n   837\tRisks include divergence from front-end tail flags, exact scalar spelling,\n   838\tnamed-VM environment leakage, malformed bound operands, source-map loss, and\n   839\tUCF's deferred transport contract.\n   840\t\n   841\tDECIDED:\n   842\t\n   843\t1. D1 normalizer: adopt recursive ex-data normalization, closure type and\n   844\t   arity comparison, continuation and parked type comparison, stream/cursor\n   845\t   ids, host-handle-free stores, and telemetry exclusion. This serves\n   846\t   interpretation parity without inventing hidden state.\n   847\t2. D2 lossless DAG: drop it from B0 through B7 and make it a non-goal. Named\n   848\t   datoms remain authoritative; lexical positions and diagnostics are derived.\n   849\t   This serves \"derive, don't persist\" and removes redundant authority.\n   850\t3. D3 descriptor: adopt the arity, slots, raw-Bytes encoding, scalar domain,\n   851\t   and lift to `:yin.code/*`. Unsupported host classes refuse before running.\n   852\t4. D4 environment leak: `yin.vm.semantic` writes the callee's merged env into\n   853\t   `:env` on return and park (`semantic.cljc:285-293, 317`); `vm-load-program`\n   854\t   does not reset it (`semantic.cljc:679-690`), while `vm-eval` restores the\n   855\t   initial env (`semantic.cljc:779`). Treat this as a separate named-VM defect\n   856\t   outside these phases; retain the fixture restriction until fixed. The fix\n   857\t   requires its own design.\n   858\t5. D5 hash identity: `:call-hash H` names the canonical executable image hash\n   859\t   of a definition unit; a recursive component is one hashed unit. H is not a\n   860\t   projection identity.\n   861\t6. D6 linker principles: the name environment is a value or stream, trust and\n   862\t   provenance are composition policy, SCCs hash as units with canonical member\n   863\t   ordering, and retry, timeout, and absence are stream events. The boundary\n   864\t   is always `dao.stream`, whether local or remote.\n   865\t7. D7 end state: both VMs coexist. B3 reports benchmark numbers only; the\n   866\t   semantic VM is never retired without its own design.\n   867\t8. D8 committed B6: closed images are fetched by H over `dao.stream`, verified\n   868\t   with `image-hash`, and loaded only after verification. This directly serves\n   869\t   invariant I.\n   870\t9. D9 H function: B1's single `image-hash` function hashes the descriptor\n   871\t   hash, including the lowering-contract version, and the canonical positional\n   872\t   vector. Jing addresses are storage locations, not H.\n   873\t10. D10 scalar classes: classes are derived from hashed constant tags; a host\n   874\t    lacking one refuses before execution. No redundant class manifest exists.\n   875\t11. D11 free names: closed means the receiver closure check passes against\n   876\t    its primitive/module tables with no free-env or store shadowing. B6 refuses\n   877\t    `:unresolved-free` and `:shadowed-free`; an image with no free operands\n   878\t    passes trivially.\n   879\t12. D12 sharing identity: H is the only identity on the sharing path. The\n   880\t    merged projection remains dormant, untouched, and has no new dependents.\n   881\t    This serves invariant I and \"derive, do not persist\" because H is already\n   882\t    alpha-invariant for binder names.\n   883\t13. D13 dependency order: B6 depends on B1, B2, and the lift, not B3 or B4;\n   884\t    the existing semantic VM can execute a fetched image after lifting. This\n   885\t    serves invariant I by making sharing available before the new VM kernel.\n   886\t14. D14 wire verification: receivers verify received content before\n   887\t    trusting it, and the descriptor hash includes the lowering-contract\n   888\t    version. Under the B6 Jing path this is two checks on the received\n   889\t    value, `segment-key` against its address and `image-hash` against H,\n   890\t    before validation or load. This serves content integrity and\n   891\t    host-boundary safety.\n   892\t15. D15 receiver closure: free names are derived by scanning operands and are\n   893\t    accepted only when primitive/module resolution is unshadowed. This serves\n   894\t    explicit state and prevents same-H semantic drift.\n   895\t16. D16 index ownership: the composition holds the H index as an explicit\n   896\t    value or as H-to-address datoms it queries; the content itself is\n   897\t    served by `dao.jing`'s existing DHT and remote server side, and B6 has\n   898\t    no responder of its own. This serves the no-global-state and\n   899\t    no-callback invariants.\n   900\t\n   901\tDEFERRED:\n   902\t\n   903\t- B6 request/response schema and contract stamps.\n   904\t- B7 dependency-closure and SCC manifest mechanics.\n   905\t- The exact retry, timeout, and permanent-absence event vocabulary.\n   906\t- A named-VM environment-leak fix design, which owns D4's release condition.\n   907\t- If the merged projection is ever retired, move the public `resolve-name`\n   908\t  helper into `yin.vm.debruijn-resolve` before removal. Dormant means kept,\n   909\t  not removed.\n   910\t\n   911\tNo owner decision blocks B0. B0 starts with D1 and the frozen parity corpus.\n   912\t\n   913\t## 9. What this design must not change\n   914\t\n   915\tThe AST schema, AST walker, named emitter, named linearizer, semantic VM,\n   916\texisting `:yin.code/*` dimension, `dao.stream` protocols, lease layer, waitset,\n   917\tnamed storage, and the merged projection namespace and pinned fingerprint\n   918\tremain unchanged. The projection is dormant and has no new dependents. The\n   919\texecutable encoding and VM are additional artifacts.\n   100\t\n   101\tR is alpha-invariant for binder renames for the same reason H is: the\n   102\tregister vector carries `:load-bound` depth and position operands and\n   103\t`:closure` arities, and binder names live in a diagnostic side table outside\n   104\tthe hash. Equal R therefore implies agreement on exact scalar spelling, free\n   105\tname operands, and front-end tail flags, as equal H does. Each format is\n   106\tdeterministically derived from the resolved tuples, so equal resolved\n   107\ttuples give equal H and equal R, and a binder rename changes neither. No\n   108\tbiconditional between H and R is claimed: two deterministic projections may\n   109\tpreserve different distinctions, and the corpus tests are evidence for the\n   110\tone-directional law only.\n   111\t\n   112\tThis is not a second semantic answer to alpha-equivalence. A register\n   113\tlowerer cannot repair differences in exact scalar spelling, free names, or\n   114\tfront-end tail flags; those differences already make the resolved tuples,\n   115\tand so both H and R, differ.\n   116\t\n   117\tThe pairing of an R with the H of the same named root is a composition\n   118\tfact, carried beside a request or recorded as a datom by whoever publishes\n   119\tboth, never inside either preimage. A receiver verifies a fetched register\n   120\timage with R alone and never needs to have seen the stack image.\n   121\t\n   122\t## 2. Compilation topology and stream contract\n   123\t\n   124\tThe register projection is a peer of the stack projection. Both branch from\n   125\tthe resolved tuples, the de Bruijn encoding of the named AST datoms:\n   267\t    | Scalar encoding      | Reuse B1 scalar bytes, never projection NFC   |\n   268\t    | Lexical addressing   | :load-bound depth/position remains explicit   |\n   269\t    | Free addressing      | :load-free name remains exact                 |\n   270\t    | Live sets            | In-band boundary operands, hashed and        |\n   271\t    |                      | recomputed by the validator (section 4.5)     |\n   272\t    | Validation           | Shape, targets, body scope, register bounds,  |\n   273\t    |                      | live-set shape, bounds, tail, and exactness   |\n   274\t    | Lift                 | Register image to named semantics             |\n   275\t    +----------------------+-----------------------------------------------+\n   276\t\n   277\tThe register descriptor has its own validator because register operands and\n   278\tbody register counts are new facts. It reuses B1's public data rules and\n   279\tscalar encoder only through an explicit dependency; it does not call private\n   280\tprojection helpers. The descriptor version is incremented whenever opcode\n   281\tshape, allocation, scalar framing, or control-flow rules change.\n   282\t\n   283\tThe lowerer's R formula is:\n   284\t\n   285\t    R = sha256(register-descriptor-hash || canonical-register-vector)\n   286\t\n   287\tBoth components are canonical bytes. R is an executable-format identity\n   288\tpinned to explicit SHA-256 by VM contract freeze, completely decoupled from\n   289\tDaoJing's `default-hash-algorithm`. A receiver verifies R over the\n   290\treceived image before trusting it, as B1's wire rule requires for H; the\n   291\tdescriptor hash and contract version are inside R, so descriptor\n   292\tagreement is that same check. It then validates, runs the receiver\n   293\tclosure check over `:load-free` operands exactly as D11 and D15 define it\n   294\tfor stack images, and only then executes. No relation to a stack H is\n   295\tembedded or checked.\n   296\t\n   297\tThe lift goes from the register image to the named `:yin.code/*` image, so\n   298\tthat B6's rule \"a fetched image may be executed by the semantic VM after\n   299\tlifting\" holds for register images too. It is a function of the register\n   300\timage plus its side table, under the stack design's trust rule (section\n  1040\tThe register lowerer consumes the resolved tuples, never the stack image and\n  1041\tnever named datoms directly. It is not a second stack lowerer: it emits only\n  1042\t`:yin.debruijn.register/*` and B2 remains the sole producer of\n  1043\t`:yin.debruijn.code/*`. Its input remains available after the transform.\n  1044\tRegister code is not portable to a stack-only host; such a host obtains a\n  1045\tstack image for the same program by H or by lowering the named datoms.\n  1046\t\n  1047\tThe resolver is not a second lossless source view. It has no identity, is\n  1048\tnot persisted, and is not a fetch key; its inverse exists for tests only.\n  1049\t\n  1050\t## 8. Risks and decisions\n  1051\t\n  1052\tRisks include register-allocation drift, spill policy becoming observable,\n  1053\teffect ordering, register continuation shape, drift between the stack and\n  1054\tregister lowerers, and the permanent second-evaluator maintenance surface\n  1055\taccepted in DECIDED 1.\n  1056\t\n  1057\tDECIDED:\n  1058\t\n  1059\t1. R0-R2 are lowerer and format work. R4, the register kernel, is\n  1060\t   authorized to proceed as soon as its prerequisites exist (R1 for the\n  1061\t   pure-program tier, R2 and B4's engine seam for the effects tier) and is\n  1062\t   not contingent on R3's report or any benchmark. This is a deliberate\n  1063\t   architectural commitment to a second evaluator, made by the owner on\n  1064\t   2026-09-23 after the tradeoff was explained: every future opcode, effect,\n  1065\t   or contract change is implemented and tested twice, in the stack kernel\n  1066\t   and the register kernel, across JVM, CLJS, and CLJD, permanently. The\n  1067\t   owner accepted that cost because the kernel is the end-to-end proof that\n  1068\t   the `dao.stream` compilation pipeline is genuinely configurable with\n  1069\t   swappable peer backends (section 1). The commitment is not informational\n  1070\t   and is not revisited by R3's numbers.\n  1071\t2. The stack image and the register image are peer projections of the\n  1072\t   resolved tuples. H and R are peer executable-format identities; neither\n  1073\t   preimage includes the other, and neither is a replacement semantic\n  1074\t   identity.\n  1075\t3. The resolved tuples are a derived stage value with a shape contract and a\n  1076\t   namespace but no hash, no identity, and no sharing role, per invariant I,\n  1077\t   D9, D12, and \"derive, do not persist\".\n  1078\t4. Lowering is a pure, non-destructive stream transform. Named datoms,\n  1079\t   resolved tuples, stack images, and register images coexist.\n  1080\t5. Lexical depth and position remain explicit. Locals and temporaries use\n  1081\t   disjoint register banks.\n  1082\t6. Allocation and any move order are canonicalized by evaluation and\n  1083\t   virtual-id order, with a versioned descriptor and golden fixtures.\n  1084\t7. The register kernel is a sibling and cannot silently replace the stack\n  1085\t   VM or alter its protocols.\n  1086\t8. B2 is the resolver-and-stack-lowerer split (section 2.2); the address\n  1087\t   law over the parity corpus remains a cross-check, not the coupling.\n  1088\t9. Live sets are in-band on every continuation boundary, as strictly\n  1089\t   ascending vectors computed by one exported backward-dataflow function,\n  1090\t   inside R's preimage, and recomputed by every receiver. R2 is register\n  1091\t   contract version 3. A saved continuation carries only live registers.\n  1092\t10. R2 defines effect opcodes, sparse continuation payloads, wait-entry\n  1093\t    shapes, and validation as pure data before R4 interprets them. The\n  1094\t    shared engine remains the sole owner of scheduling and wake disposition.\n  1095\t\n  1096\tDEFERRED:\n  1097\t\n  1098\t- Spill representation and register-file limits, if a target requires them.\n  1099\t- Register continuation lifting and cross-model park/resume transport.\n  1100\t- The B7 name-environment ledger and provenance that the R5 same-root\n  1101\t  pairing's trust rests on; R4 and R5 are both decided phases.\n  1102\t\n  1103\t## 9. End condition\n  1104\t\n  1105\tThe lowerer-only end condition is a validated, deterministic register image\n  1106\twhose R is reproducible on all three hosts, whose address law with the stack\n  1107\timage holds over the parity corpus, and whose named-VM normalized fixtures\n  1108\tagree. The full register-VM end condition adds a validator-approved kernel\n  1109\twith both R4 tiers complete, the R3 report published for the owner to read,\n  1110\tand, if R5 is commissioned, cross-stream R fetch and verification. The\n  1111\tproject does not stop at R2: R3's numbers inform, they do not decide.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
