{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"agent_message","text":"Completed-GMT: 2026-09-23 18:59:25 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-09-24 01:59:25 +0700 ICT\n\n**REQUEST CHANGES**\n\n1. **P1 | [yin.vm.debruijn.linker.md:208](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.linker.md:208) | Address verification still uses the minting default.**  \n   `(jing/segment-key value)` selects BLAKE3, regardless of the requested address’s algorithm. A valid SHA-256 address therefore fails this comparison, contradicting lines 230–233. [dao.jing:406](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:406) confirms that behavior. Replace the predicate with `(not (jing/segment-matches? address value))` and require successful fetch coverage for both registered address algorithms.\n\n2. **P2 | [yin.vm.debruijn.linker.md:477](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.linker.md:477) | The completion criterion overstates independence from handle verification.**  \n   A DHT peer’s mismatched payload is discarded inside [make-get:218](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/dht.cljc:218); if no valid peer answers, the handle returns absence. The linker never receives that payload to diagnose its mismatch. Preserve the original requirement—wrong peer content is rejected before load on both topologies—and separately test the linker’s own check using corrupted DHT-local content and a corrupt client response. Permit `:absent` for peer content filtered by the DHT.\n\nThe requested properties otherwise pass:\n\n- **BLAKE3 correction approved in substance:** addresses use the selected algorithm over UTF-8 bytes of the order-normalized print. H and R remain explicitly SHA-256 and independent of storage encoding. No remaining SHA-256 reference incorrectly describes `segment-key`.\n- **Extraction is substantially faithful:** the shared fetch function, refusal rules, closure checks, descriptor binding, migration risk, and dependency boundaries are preserved.\n- **Address versus H/R:** both checks remain mandatory and protect different preimages and claims.\n- **Same-root pairing:** root-associated datoms, explicit trusted fallback, local re-lowering for verification, comparison of both hashes, and `:pairing-mismatch` are accurately preserved.\n\nRead-only review; no files edited. Runtime tests were not run for this documentation-only change."}}
